{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/service at exact clean pushed head e2519296108c961d930c63f2978a5560d464ea58 on request/resident-waiter-bound, answering 118679d3 under my promise a5cabc0d. This is the implementation report; there is no separate readiness report to follow.\n\nWHAT IS THERE. Both long-poll routes funnel through one handler, handleWaitResponse, so the bound goes there and there is exactly one of it: a waitSlots channel on Server with capacity DefaultWaitConcurrency, which is 64. The acquire is a select with a default arm, so a spent budget answers at once instead of queueing; the caller is a long poller with its own retry schedule, and queueing a refusal would hold the resource the bound exists to protect. The release is a deferred receive placed immediately after the acquire, which is what makes completion, client cancellation, error and panic all give the slot back.\n\nThe two routes share one budget rather than carrying one each because they consume the same thing — a goroutine ticking the snapshot. Split per route, the combined load reaches twice the number the operator was told.\n\nThe 429 uses the envelope already in this file: Content-Type, Cache-Control no-store, and a map with one error key, the same shape handleOrientation writes for its 404 and TrustedHostHandler writes for its loopback refusal. I checked those call sites rather than inventing a form. New() delegates to NewObserved(), so there is no constructor path that leaves the channel nil, which would have blocked forever instead of refusing.\n\nNothing else moved. Request-body limits, nexus resources, loopback and mutation-host guards, header behaviour, and the observer and projection paths are untouched, no configuration surface was added, and none of the obsolete a63c378a Limits plumbing came back.\n\nTESTS, AND WHAT KILLS THEM. Three tests in internal/service/wait_bound_test.go. Four mutations were applied, each confirmed to have actually landed by reading git diff and confirmed to compile before the tests were run, then reverted with the baseline re-verified green:\n\n  Dropping the deferred release: all three fail, each with slots stuck at its capacity.\n  Giving each route its own channel: the cross-route test fails, actor-wait answering 200 while wait held the whole budget. The cancellation test passes under this one because it only uses /v0/wait. Reported rather than hidden.\n  Making the acquire block instead of refusing: all three fail, the held tests on the probe timeout.\n  Raising the bound to 1\u003c\u003c20: only the 64-saturation test fails, which is the one test that exercises the default rather than a narrowed seam.\n\nGATES ACTUALLY RUN, at this exact head, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty and go vet ./... clean, both of which I re-ran myself. Full go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, thirty packages ok and no FAIL line anywhere in the log, including cmd/gs at 141s, internal/kernel at 144s and internal/app at 99s. internal/service 21.9s standalone.\n\nWHAT I LEFT OUT, DELIBERATELY. The refusal returns before the handler starts measuring, so a tripping bound records nothing through the observer. That matches the existing decode-error path, which also records nothing, but it does mean an operator cannot see the bound working from the observations alone. I judged it outside both the conditions and the instruction to keep this small, and I would rather you decide that than find it. There is no Retry-After header, because no handler here sets advisory headers and I did not want to introduce the first one on a guard.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The bound is entirely inside internal/service, the composition and transport layer in docs/reference/architecture.md. No layer contract changes, so that page is not touched.","body":{"commit":"e2519296108c961d930c63f2978a5560d464ea58","path":"internal/service"}}